The CG-mounted baseline has pooled 3-D displacement RMS of
\SI{0.405}{m} and maximum pooled
roll/pitch RMS of \SI{0.251}{\degree}.
Table~\ref{tab:imu-lever-arm} reports the worst canonical direction at each
installation distance.  At \SI{30}{cm} the rotational term
reaches \SI{0.116}{\meter\per\second\squared} RMS in the worst
direction. The largest unmodeled 3-D displacement ratio is
\num{1.003} times the CG
baseline in the port direction, and the largest tilt
ratio is \num{0.988} in the
port direction.  Modelling the same lever arm inside
the filter subtracts its rotational force: with exact angular kinematics the largest 3-D ratio over
the three directions is \num{1.000}, and with the deployable
model driven by the measured rate it is
\num{1.001}, leaving only
\SI{0.022}{\meter\per\second\squared} of the injected term
behind. The attitude channel is reported separately:
the worst tilt ratio is \num{1.000} under the exact model
and \num{1.009} under the deployable one.  The installation penalty
depends on the interaction with existing residuals; force subtraction need not improve every error metric.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Worst pooled degradation over the three canonical IMU lever-arm directions.  Ratios are relative to the CG-mounted OU--III baseline; the parenthesized direction is the maximizing unmodeled case.  The last two columns are the worst ratio over the same three directions once the lever arm is modelled inside the filter.}
  \label{tab:imu-lever-arm}
  \footnotesize
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.0pt}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}rrrrr@{}}
    \toprule
    Offset & Max 3-D / CG & Max tilt / CG & Gyro model & Exact model \\
    {[cm]} & unmodeled & unmodeled & max 3-D / CG & max 3-D / CG \\
    \midrule
    10 & 1.001 (port) & 0.998 (port) & 1.000 & 1.000 \\
    20 & 1.002 (port) & 0.994 (port) & 1.001 & 1.000 \\
    30 & 1.003 (port) & 0.988 (port) & 1.001 & 1.000 \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The deployable model has one design parameter, the band of its rate
derivative, and it is two-sided.  Over the same eight seas with a
\SI{30}{cm} port arm, a
\SI{1}{Hz} corner leaves
\num{1.013} times the CG
baseline because the low-pass phase lag misaligns a correction whose
amplitude is already right, and a \SI{100}{Hz}
corner leaves \num{1.025} because
the differentiated gyro noise it admits exceeds the term it removes.  The
basin between them is flat: the deployed
\SI{15}{Hz} corner reaches
\num{1.000} and the best
corner measured, \SI{5}{Hz}, reaches
\num{0.999}, so the model is
robust to this choice rather than tuned to it.
